From: Paul-Louis Charrière <plcharriere@gmail.com>
Date: Mon, 28 Sep 2026 20:07:00 +0900
Subject: [PATCH] netfilter: limit bridge FDB cleanup to actual moves

A SWITCHDEV_FDB_DEL_TO_DEVICE notification does not identify roaming:
bridge ageing, flush and port removal emit the same event. Listen for FDB
additions instead and compare the current bridge FDB with the bridge egress
port cached when each hardware flow was constructed. Tear a flow down only
when its destination MAC is now learned on a different bridge port.

Coalesce any number of FDB additions into one current-state rescan. This
uses bounded memory, cannot lose a move to atomic allocation failure and
avoids doing one full flowtable walk per learning event. Ignore software
flows and bridge FDB deletions.

Neighbour-output routes name the bridge master rather than the selected
bridge port. Add an RCU-safe bridge helper which returns the port only when
all non-local FDB entries for the MAC agree, and cache that port and bridge
ifindex together with the destination MAC while constructing the rule. This
also lets the rescan identify a stale rule after the old Wi-Fi netdev or
its neighbour entry has disappeared. Ambiguous and absent FDB state is
ignored.

Keep the existing workqueue drain that prevents a late hardware REPLACE
from undoing cleanup.

Assisted-by: Codex:gpt-5.6-sol
Signed-off-by: Paul-Louis Charrière <plcharriere@gmail.com>
---
